{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nr=json.load(open('../research.json'))\nfor k in ['audit_spec_version','pending_work','capacity']:print(k,json.dumps(r.get(k)))\nPY\nsed -n '173,247p' ../agent-source/doc.go\n grep -R -n 'LocalApplyThread\\\\|MsgStorageApply' ../agent-source --include='*.go' | head -55\"","aggregated_output":"\nThe library can be configured with an alternate interface for local storage\nwrites that can provide better performance in the presence of high proposal\nconcurrency by minimizing interference between proposals. This feature is called\nAsynchronousStorageWrites, and can be enabled using the flag on the Config\nstruct with the same name.\n\nWhen Asynchronous Storage Writes is enabled, the responsibility of code using\nthe library is different from what was presented above. Users still read from\nthe Node.Ready() channel. However, they process the updates it contains in a\ndifferent manner. Users no longer consult the HardState, Entries, and Snapshot\nfields (steps 1 and 3 above). They also no longer call Node.Advance() to\nindicate that they have processed all entries in the Ready (step 4 above).\nInstead, all local storage operations are also communicated through messages\npresent in the Ready.Message slice.\n\nThe local storage messages come in two flavors. The first flavor is log append\nmessages, which target a LocalAppendThread and carry Entries, HardState, and a\nSnapshot. The second flavor is entry application messages, which target a\nLocalApplyThread and carry CommittedEntries. Messages to the same target must be\nreliably processed in order. Messages to different targets can be processed in\nany order.\n\nEach local storage message carries a slice of response messages that must\ndelivered after the corresponding storage write has been completed. These\nresponses may target the same node or may target other nodes.\n\nWith Asynchronous Storage Writes enabled, the total state machine handling loop\nwill look something like this:\n\n\tfor {\n\t  select {\n\t  case <-s.Ticker:\n\t    n.Tick()\n\t  case rd := <-s.Node.Ready():\n\t    for _, m := range rd.Messages {\n\t      switch m.To {\n\t      case raft.LocalAppendThread:\n\t        toAppend <- m\n\t      case raft.LocalApplyThread:\n\t        toApply <-m\n\t      default:\n\t        sendOverNetwork(m)\n\t      }\n\t    }\n\t  case <-s.done:\n\t    return\n\t  }\n\t}\n\nUsage of Asynchronous Storage Writes will typically also contain a pair of\nstorage handler threads, one for log writes (append) and one for entry\napplication to the local state machine (apply). Those will look something like:\n\n\t// append thread\n\tgo func() {\n\t  for {\n\t    select {\n\t    case m := <-toAppend:\n\t      saveToStorage(m.State, m.Entries, m.Snapshot)\n\t      send(m.Responses)\n\t    case <-s.done:\n\t      return\n\t    }\n\t  }\n\t}\n\n\t// apply thread\n\tgo func() {\n\t  for {\n\t    select {\n\t    case m := <-toApply:\n\t      for _, entry := range m.CommittedEntries {\n\t        process(entry)\n\t        if entry.Type == raftpb.EntryConfChange {\n../agent-source/rawnode.go:378:\t\tType:    pb.MsgStorageApply,\n../agent-source/rawnode.go:379:\t\tTo:      LocalApplyThread,\n../agent-source/rawnode.go:394:\t\tType:    pb.MsgStorageApplyResp,\n../agent-source/rawnode.go:396:\t\tFrom:    LocalApplyThread,\n../agent-source/raftpb/raft.pb.go:96:\tMsgStorageApply      MessageType = 21\n../agent-source/raftpb/raft.pb.go:97:\tMsgStorageApplyResp  MessageType = 22\n../agent-source/raftpb/raft.pb.go:123:\t21: \"MsgStorageApply\",\n../agent-source/raftpb/raft.pb.go:124:\t22: \"MsgStorageApplyResp\",\n../agent-source/raftpb/raft.pb.go:150:\t\"MsgStorageApply\":      21,\n../agent-source/raftpb/raft.pb.go:151:\t\"MsgStorageApplyResp\":  22,\n../agent-source/raftpb/raft.pb.go:433:\t// is complete. Populated for MsgStorageAppend and MsgStorageApply messages.\n../agent-source/node_test.go:891:\t\tcase raftpb.MsgStorageApply:\n../agent-source/node_test.go:929:\t\tcase raftpb.MsgStorageApply:\n../agent-source/node_test.go:951:\t\tcase raftpb.MsgStorageApply:\n../agent-source/node_test.go:967:\t\t\t\trequire.NotEqual(t, raftpb.MsgStorageApply, m.Type, \"unexpected message: %v\", m)\n../agent-source/node_test.go:982:\trequire.Equal(t, raftpb.MsgStorageApply, m.Type)\n../agent-source/doc.go:192:LocalApplyThread and carry CommittedEntries. Messages to the same target must be\n../agent-source/doc.go:212:\t      case raft.LocalApplyThread:\n../agent-source/doc.go:394:\t'MsgStorageApply' is a message from a node to its local apply storage\n../agent-source/doc.go:396:\twhich will be a 'MsgStorageApplyResp' back to itself. Used with\n../agent-source/raft.go:41:\t// LocalApplyThread is a reference to a local thread that applies committed\n../agent-source/raft.go:43:\t// target for MsgStorageApply messages when AsyncStorageWrites is enabled.\n../agent-source/raft.go:44:\tLocalApplyThread uint64 = math.MaxUint64 - 1\n../agent-source/raft.go:161:\t// MsgStorageApply messages. The messages will target a LocalAppendThread\n../agent-source/raft.go:162:\t// and a LocalApplyThread, respectively. Messages to the same target must be\n../agent-source/raft.go:174:\t// MsgStorageApply carries committed entries to apply. Writes performed in\n../agent-source/raft.go:175:\t// service of a MsgStorageApply need not be durable before response messages\n../agent-source/raft.go:198:\t// MsgStorageApply messages when AsyncStorageWrites is enabled.\n../agent-source/raft.go:1197:\tcase pb.MsgStorageApplyResp:\n../agent-source/rafttest/interaction_env_handler_process_ready.go:68:\t\t\tcase raftpb.MsgStorageApply:\n../agent-source/rafttest/interaction_env.go:43:\tApplyWork  []pb.Message // []MsgStorageApply\n../agent-source/util.go:37:\tpb.MsgStorageApply:      true,\n../agent-source/util.go:38:\tpb.MsgStorageApplyResp:  true,\n../agent-source/util.go:49:\tpb.MsgStorageApp